(Designing object-oriented software is hard, and designing )Tj
/T1_1 1 Tf
(reusable)Tj
/T1_0 1 Tf
( object-oriented software is even harder. )Tj
0 -1.2 TD
(You must find pertinent objects, factor them into classes at the right g\ranularity, define class interfaces and )Tj
T*
(inheritance hierarchies, and establish key relationships among them. You\r design should be specific to the )Tj
T*
(problem at hand but also general enough to address future problems and r\equirements. You also want to )Tj
T*
(avoid redesign, or at least minimize it. Experienced object-oriented des\igners will tell you that a reusable )Tj
T*
(and flexible design is difficult if not impossible to get "right" the fi\rst time. Before a design is finished, they )Tj
T*
(usually try to reuse it several times, modifying it each time.)Tj
0 -2.557 TD
(Yet experienced object-oriented designers do make good designs. Meanwhil\e new designers are )Tj
0 -1.2 TD
(overwhelmed by the options available and tend to fall back on non-object\-oriented techniques they've used )Tj
T*
(before. It takes a long time for novices to learn what good object-orien\ted design is all about. Experienced )Tj
T*
(designers evidently know something inexperienced ones don't. What is it?\)Tj
0 -2.557 TD
(One thing expert designers know )Tj
/T1_1 1 Tf
(not)Tj
/T1_0 1 Tf
( to do is solve every problem from first principles. Rather, they reuse \)Tj
0 -1.2 TD
(solutions that have worked for them in the past. When they find a good s\olution, they use it again and again. )Tj
T*
(Such experience is part of what makes them experts. Consequently, you'll\ find recurring patterns of classes )Tj
T*
(and communicating objects in many object-oriented systems. These pattern\s solve specific design problems )Tj
T*
(and make object-oriented designs more flexible, elegant, and ultimately \reusable. They help designers reuse )Tj
T*
(successful designs by basing new designs on prior experience. A designer\ who is familiar with such patterns )Tj
T*
(can apply them immediately to design problems without having to rediscov\er them.)Tj
0 -2.557 TD
(An analogy will help illustrate the point. Novelists and playwrights rar\ely design their plots from scratch. )Tj
0 -1.2 TD
(Instead, they follow patterns like "Tragically Flawed Hero" \(Macbeth, H\amlet, etc.\) or "The Romantic )Tj
T*
(Novel" \(countless romance novels\). In the same way, object-oriented de\signers follow patterns like )Tj
T*
("represent states with objects" and "decorate objects so you can easily \add/remove features." Once you )Tj
T*
(know the pattern, a lot of design decisions follow automatically.)Tj
0 -2.557 TD
(We all know the value of design experience. How many times have you had \design )Tj
/T1_1 1 Tf
(d\351j\340-vu)Tj
/T1_0 1 Tf
(\227that feeling )Tj
0 -1.2 TD
(that you've solved a problem before but not knowing exactly where or how\? If you could remember the )Tj
0 -1.2 TD
(details of the previous problem and how you solved it, then you could re\use the experience instead of )Tj
0 -1.2 TD
(rediscovering it. However, we don't do a good job of recording experienc\e in software design for others to )Tj
0 -1.2 TD
(use.)Tj
0 -2.557 TD
(The purpose of this book is to record experience in designing object-ori\ented software as )Tj
