(explicit specification of class and object interactions and their underl\ying intent. Put simply, design patterns )Tj
0 -1.2 TD
(help a designer get a design "right" faster.)Tj
0 -2.557 TD
(None of the design patterns in this book describes new or unproven desig\ns. We have included only designs )Tj
0 -1.2 TD
(that have been applied more than once in different systems. Most of thes\e designs have never been )Tj
T*
(documented before. They are either part of the folklore of the object-or\iented community or are elements of )Tj
T*
(some successful object-oriented systems\227neither of which is easy for \novice designers to learn from. So )Tj
T*
(although these designs aren't new, we capture them in a new and accessib\le way: as a catalog of design )Tj
T*
(patterns having a consistent format.)Tj
0 -2.557 TD
(Despite the book's size, the design patterns in it capture only a fracti\on of what an expert might know. It )Tj
0 -1.2 TD
(doesn't have any patterns dealing with concurrency or distributed progra\mming or real-time programming. )Tj
T*
(It doesn't have any application domain-specific patterns. It doesn't tel\l you how to build user interfaces, how )Tj
T*
(to write device drivers, or how to use an object-oriented database. Each\ of these areas has its own patterns, )Tj
T*
(and it would be worthwhile for someone to catalog those too.)Tj
