0 -2.557 TD
(A design pattern names, abstracts, and identifies the key aspects of a c\ommon design structure that make it )Tj
0 -1.2 TD
(useful for creating a reusable object-oriented design. The design patter\n identifies the participating classes )Tj
T*
(and instances, their roles and collaborations, and the distribution of r\esponsibilities. Each design pattern )Tj
T*
(focuses on a particular object-oriented design problem or issue. It desc\ribes when it applies, whether it can )Tj
T*
(be applied in view of other design constraints, and the consequences and\ trade-offs of its use. Since we must )Tj
T*
(eventually implement our designs, a design pattern also provides sample \C++ and \(sometimes\) Smalltalk )Tj
T*
(code to illustrate an implementation.)Tj
0 -2.557 TD
(Although design patterns describe object-oriented designs, they are base\d on practical solutions that have )Tj
0 -1.2 TD
(been implemented in mainstream object-oriented programming languages lik\e Smalltalk and C++ rather )Tj
T*
(than procedural languages \(Pascal, C, Ada\) or more dynamic object-orie\nted languages \(CLOS, Dylan, )Tj
0 -1.2 TD
(Self\). We chose Smalltalk and C++ for pragmatic reasons: Our day-to-day\ experience has been in these )Tj
0 -1.2 TD
(languages, and they are increasingly popular.)Tj
0 -2.557 TD
(The choice of programming language is important because it influences on\e's point of view. Our patterns )Tj
0 -1.2 TD
(assume Smalltalk/C++-level language features, and that choice determines\ what can and cannot be )Tj
T*
(implemented easily. If we assumed procedural languages, we might have in\cluded design patterns called )Tj
T*
("Inheritance," "Encapsulation," and "Polymorphism." Similarly, some of o\ur patterns are supported directly )Tj
T*
(by the less common object-oriented languages. CLOS has multi-methods, fo\r example, which lessen the )Tj
T*
(need for a pattern such as )Tj
