(Looking at the design patterns inside MVC should help you see what we me\an by the term "pattern.")Tj
0 -2.557 TD
(MVC consists of three kinds of objects. The Model is the application obj\ect, the View is its screen )Tj
0 -1.2 TD
(presentation, and the Controller defines the way the user interface reac\ts to user input. Before MVC, user )Tj
T*
(interface designs tended to lump these objects together. MVC decouples t\hem to increase flexibility and )Tj
T*
(reuse.)Tj
0 -2.557 TD
(MVC decouples views and models by establishing a subscribe/notify protoc\ol between them. A view must )Tj
0 -1.2 TD
(ensure that its appearance reflects the state of the model. Whenever the\ model's data changes, the model )Tj
T*
(notifies views that depend on it. In response, each view gets an opportu\nity to update itself. This approach )Tj
T*
(lets you attach multiple views to a model to provide different presentat\ions. You can also create new views )Tj
T*
(for a model without rewriting it.)Tj
0 -2.557 TD
(The following diagram shows a model and three views. \(We've left out th\e controllers for simplicity.\) The )Tj
0 -1.2 TD
(model contains some data values, and the views defining a spreadsheet, h\istogram, and pie chart display )Tj
T*
(these data in various ways. The model communicates with its views when i\ts values change, and the views )Tj
T*
(communicate with the model to access these values.)Tj
ET
