0 0 0 rg
( design pattern. It lets you create a class hierarchy in which some subc\lasses define )Tj
0 -1.343 TD
(primitive objects \(e.g., Button\) and other classes define composite ob\jects \(CompositeView\) that assemble )Tj
0 -1.2 TD
(the primitives into more complex objects.)Tj
0 -2.557 TD
(MVC also lets you change the way a view responds to user input without c\hanging its visual presentation. )Tj
0 -1.2 TD
(You might want to change the way it responds to the keyboard, for exampl\e, or have it use a pop-up menu )Tj
T*
(instead of command keys. MVC encapsulates the response mechanism in a Co\ntroller object. There is a )Tj
T*
(class hierarchy of controllers, making it easy to create a new controlle\r as a variation on an existing one.)Tj
0 -2.557 TD
(A view uses an instance of a Controller subclass to implement a particul\ar response strategy; to implement a )Tj
0 -1.2 TD
(different strategy, simply replace the instance with a different kind of\ controller. It's even possible to change )Tj
T*
(a view's controller at run-time to let the view change the way it respon\ds to user input. For example, a view )Tj
T*
(can be disabled so that it doesn't accept input simply by giving it a co\ntroller that ignores input events.)Tj
0 -2.557 TD
