(The second criterion, called )Tj
/T1_1 1 Tf
(scope)Tj
/T1_0 1 Tf
(, specifies whether the pattern applies primarily to classes or to objec\ts. )Tj
T*
(Class patterns deal with relationships between classes and their subclas\ses. These relationships are )Tj
0 -1.2 TD
(established through inheritance, so they are static\227fixed at compile-\time. Object patterns deal with object )Tj
0 -1.2 TD
(relationships, which can be changed at run-time and are more dynamic. Al\most all patterns use inheritance )Tj
0 -1.2 TD
(to some extent. So the only patterns labeled "class patterns" are those \that focus on class relationships. Note )Tj
0 -1.2 TD
(that most patterns are in the Object scope.)Tj
0 -2.557 TD
(Creational class patterns defer some part of object creation to subclass\es, while Creational object patterns )Tj
0 -1.2 TD
(defer it to another object. The Structural class patterns use inheritanc\e to compose classes, while the )Tj
T*
(Structural object patterns describe ways to assemble objects. The Behavi\oral class patterns use inheritance )Tj
T*
(to describe algorithms and flow of control, whereas the Behavioral objec\t patterns describe how a group of )Tj
T*
(objects cooperate to perform a task that no single object can carry out \alone.)Tj
0 -2.557 TD
(There are other ways to organize the patterns. Some patterns are often u\sed together. For example, )Tj
0 -1.2 TD
(Composite is often used with Iterator or Visitor. Some patterns are alte\rnatives: Prototype is often an )Tj
T*
(alternative to Abstract Factory. Some patterns result in similar designs\ even though the patterns have )Tj
T*
(different intents. For example, the structure diagrams of Composite and \Decorator are similar.)Tj
0 -2.557 TD
(Yet another way to organize design patterns is according to how they ref\erence each other in their "Related )Tj
