(You must find pertinent objects, factor them into classes at the right g\ranularity, define class interfaces and )Tj
T*
(inheritance hierarchies, and establish key relationships among them. You\r design should be specific to the )Tj
0 -1.2 TD
(problem at hand but also general enough to address future problems and r\equirements. You also want to )Tj
0 -1.2 TD
(avoid redesign, or at least minimize it. Experienced object-oriented des\igners will tell you that a reusable )Tj
T*
(and flexible design is difficult if not impossible to get "right" the fi\rst time. Before a design is finished, they )Tj
T*
(usually try to reuse it several times, modifying it each time.)Tj
0 -2.557 TD
(Yet experienced object-oriented designers do make good designs. Meanwhil\e new designers are )Tj
0 -1.2 TD
(overwhelmed by the options available and tend to fall back on non-object\-oriented techniques they've used )Tj
T*
(before. It takes a long time for novices to learn what good object-orien\ted design is all about. Experienced )Tj
T*
(designers evidently know something inexperienced ones don't. What is it?\)Tj
