(A toolkit is a set of related and reusable classes designed to provide u\seful, general-purpose functionality. )Tj
0 -1.2 TD
(An example of a toolkit is a set of collection classes for lists, associ\ative tables, stacks, and the like. The C+)Tj
T*
(+ I/O stream library is another example. Toolkits don't impose a particu\lar design on your application; they )Tj
0 -1.2 TD
(just provide functionality that can help your application do its job. Th\ey let you as an implementer avoid )Tj
0 -1.2 TD
(recoding common functionality. Toolkits emphasize )Tj
/T1_1 1 Tf
(code reuse)Tj
/T1_0 1 Tf
(. They are the object-oriented equivalent of )Tj
T*
(subroutine libraries.)Tj
0 -2.557 TD
(Toolkit design is arguably harder than application design, because toolk\its have to work in many )Tj
0 -1.2 TD
(applications to be useful. Moreover, the toolkit writer isn't in a posit\ion to know what those applications )Tj
T*
(will be or their special needs. That makes it all the more important to \avoid assumptions and dependencies )Tj
T*
(that can limit the toolkit's flexibility and consequently its applicabil\ity and effectiveness.)Tj
/T1_2 1 Tf
