(]. You customize a framework to a )Tj
-25.054 -1.343 Td
(particular application by creating application-specific subclasses of ab\stract classes from the framework.)Tj
0 -2.557 TD
(The framework dictates the architecture of your application. It will def\ine the overall structure, its )Tj
0 -1.2 TD
(partitioning into classes and objects, the key responsibilities thereof,\ how the classes and objects )Tj
0 -1.2 TD
(collaborate, and the thread of control. A framework predefines these des\ign parameters so that you, the )Tj
T*
(application designer/implementer, can concentrate on the specifics of yo\ur application. The framework )Tj
0 -1.2 TD
(captures the design decisions that are common to its application domain.\ Frameworks thus emphasize )Tj
/T1_1 1 Tf
T*
(design reuse)Tj
/T1_0 1 Tf
( over code reuse, though a framework will usually include concrete subcl\asses you can put to )Tj
T*
(work immediately.)Tj
0 -2.557 TD
(Reuse on this level leads to an inversion of control between the applica\tion and the software on which it's )Tj
0 -1.2 TD
(based. When you use a toolkit \(or a conventional subroutine library for\ that matter\), you write the main )Tj
0 -1.2 TD
(body of the application and call the code you want to reuse. When you us\e a framework, you reuse the main )Tj
0 -1.2 TD
(body and write the code )Tj
/T1_1 1 Tf
(it)Tj
/T1_0 1 Tf
( calls. You'll have to write operations with particular names and callin\g )Tj
T*
(conventions, but that reduces the design decisions you have to make.)Tj
0 -2.557 TD
(Not only can you build applications faster as a result, but the applicat\ions have similar structures. They are )Tj
0 -1.2 TD
(easier to maintain, and they seem more consistent to their users. On the\ other hand, you lose some creative )Tj
T*
(freedom, since many design decisions have been made for you.)Tj
0 -2.557 TD
(If applications are hard to design, and toolkits are harder, then framew\orks are hardest of all. A framework )Tj
0 -1.2 TD
(designer gambles that one architecture will work for all applications in\ the domain. Any substantive change )Tj
T*
(to the framework's design would reduce its benefits considerably, since \the framework's main contribution )Tj
T*
(to an application is the architecture it defines. Therefore it's imperat\ive to design the framework to be as )Tj
